    "setup_id": "05601a00c6bc4954635863826740b7b61d18798ad2f99e32a9fef415a652990f",
    "text": "To solve for the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we need to analyze the given trapezoid \\(ABCD\\) with \\(\\overline{AD} \\parallel \\overline{BC}\\), \\(\\overline{AC} \\perp \\overline{CD}\\), and \\(\\overline{AC}\\) bisecting \\(\\angle BAD\\). We are also given that the area of the trapezoid \\(ABCD\\) is 42.\n\nFirst, let's denote the lengths of the bases as \\(AD = a\\) and \\(BC = b\\), and the height of the trapezoid as \\(h\\). The area of the trapezoid is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (a + b) \\times h = 42\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n(a + b) \\times h = 84\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\overline{AC}\\) is the height of the trapezoid, we have \\(h = AC\\). Also, since \\(\\overline{AC}\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAD\\), \\(\\angle BAC = \\angle CAD\\). This implies that \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is isosceles with \\(AD = AC\\).\n\nNow, consider the right triangle \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Since \\(\\overline{AC} \\perp \\overline{CD}\\), \\(\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ\\). Let \\(AD = AC = x\\). Then the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of } \\triangle ACD = \\frac{1}{2} \\times AD \\times CD = \\frac{1}{2} \\times x \\times h\n\\]\nSince \\(h = x\\) in \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of } \\triangle ACD = \\frac{1}{2} \\times x \\times x = \\frac{1}{2} x^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(x\\). Since \\(AD = x\\) and \\(CD = h = x\\), we can use the Pythagorean theorem in \\(\\triangle ACD\\):\n\\[\nAC^2 = AD^2 + CD^2 \\implies x^2 = x^2 + x^2 - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot x \\cdot \\cos(\\theta) \\implies x^2 = x^2 + x^2 - 2x^2 \\cos(\\theta) \\implies x^2 = 2x^2 (1 - \\cos(\\theta))\n\\]\nSince \\(\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{h}{x} = \\frac{x}{x} = 1\\) (because \\(\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ\\)), we have:\n\\[\nx^2 = 2x^2 (1 - 1) = 0 \\implies x^2 = \\frac{84}{2} = 42\n\\]\nThus, \\(x^2 = 21\\), and the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of } \\triangle ACD = \\frac{1}{2} x^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 21 = 21\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{21}\n\\]",
